sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/wolfram2002-ch03-22> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "A methodology based on computer experiments, in which one runs precisely specified programs and observes their behavior, can discover genuinely unexpected phenomena that a traditional approach of constructing examples of predefined behavior would miss, while giving results as precise as mathematical statements.";
    dc:type "conceptual";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Computer experiments let one discover phenomena not anticipated in advance";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Stephen Wolfram, A New Kind of Science (Champaign, IL: Wolfram Media, 2002), Chapter 3, “The World of Simple Programs”; section “How the Discoveries in This Chapter Were Made” (from p. 108), p. 108. Scope: methodology of studying simple programs"
}
